# Datasets and Benchmarks

This document contains datasets and benchmarks relevant to **Safety and Rollback Mechanisms for Objective Misinterpretation in Agentic AI Systems**.

These resources can be used to evaluate harmful behavior, objective misinterpretation, prompt injection, unsafe tool use, policy compliance, and other safety failures in autonomous AI agents.

---

## 1. Agent-SafetyBench

**Type:** Agent Safety Evaluation Benchmark

**Purpose:**
Agent-SafetyBench is designed to systematically evaluate the safety of Large Language Model (LLM) agents. It provides diverse environments and covers multiple safety risk categories and failure modes.

**Relevance to this project:**
This benchmark is highly relevant for evaluating whether an agent follows its intended objective and behaves safely when interacting with different environments and tools.

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.14470
* GitHub: https://github.com/thu-coai/Agent-SafetyBench
* Dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/thu-coai/Agent-SafetyBench

The official repository provides the benchmark data, environments, evaluation code, and scoring components.

---

## 2. AgentHarm

**Type:** Harmfulness Evaluation Benchmark for LLM Agents

**Purpose:**
AgentHarm evaluates the ability and propensity of LLM agents to complete harmful multi-step tasks.

The benchmark contains malicious agent tasks across multiple harm categories and includes augmented versions of the tasks.

**Relevance to this project:**
AgentHarm is useful for evaluating whether an autonomous agent can be induced to perform harmful actions and whether safety mechanisms can prevent such behavior.

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.09024
* Dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/ai-safety-institute/AgentHarm
* Evaluation implementation: https://github.com/UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_evals/tree/main/src/inspect_evals/agentharm

The benchmark's official dataset is distributed through Hugging Face and is designed for safety evaluation rather than general-purpose model training.

---

## 3. AgentDojo

**Type:** Agent Safety and Prompt Injection Evaluation Environment

**Purpose:**
AgentDojo is a dynamic environment designed to evaluate attacks and defenses for LLM agents.

It provides tasks involving tool use and external information, allowing researchers to study prompt injection attacks and defensive mechanisms.

**Relevance to this project:**
An agent can receive instructions from external sources that conflict with the user's original objective. AgentDojo can therefore be used to evaluate objective preservation, tool-use safety, and defensive mechanisms.

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.13352
* GitHub: https://github.com/ethz-spylab/agentdojo

The repository provides benchmark suites, task environments, attack methods, and defense mechanisms.

---

## 4. ToolEmu

**Type:** Tool-Use Safety Benchmark

**Purpose:**
ToolEmu is an LM-based emulation framework for identifying risks associated with language-model agents that use external tools.

The benchmark contains curated test cases and toolkits for evaluating agent trajectories.

**Relevance to this project:**
ToolEmu is particularly relevant because objective misinterpretation can become more dangerous when an agent has access to tools capable of modifying external systems.

It can help evaluate:

* Unsafe tool calls
* Incorrect actions
* Potential side effects
* Tool-use risks
* Agent trajectories

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.15817
* GitHub: https://github.com/ryoungj/ToolEmu

The official repository describes a curated benchmark containing **144 test cases and 36 toolkits**.

---

## 5. τ-Bench

**Type:** Tool-Agent-User Interaction Benchmark

**Purpose:**
τ-Bench evaluates interactions between users, AI agents, and domain-specific tools in realistic environments.

The benchmark provides:

* User simulations
* Agent policies
* Domain-specific tools
* Evaluation tasks
* Historical trajectories

**Relevance to this project:**
The benchmark is useful for studying whether agents correctly interpret user instructions while following domain policies and using external tools.

This makes it relevant to objective interpretation, policy compliance, and safe autonomous execution.

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045
* GitHub: https://github.com/sierra-research/tau-bench

The repository provides historical trajectories for some benchmark environments and supports multiple real-world domains.

---

## 6. InjecAgent

**Type:** Indirect Prompt Injection Benchmark

**Purpose:**
InjecAgent evaluates indirect prompt injection attacks against tool-integrated LLM agents.

The benchmark contains tasks designed to test whether external malicious instructions can cause an agent to perform actions that conflict with its intended task.

**Relevance to this project:**
This is directly related to objective misinterpretation because an agent may encounter instructions from external content and incorrectly prioritize them over the original user objective.

**Resources:**

* Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02691
* GitHub: https://github.com/uiuc-kang-lab/InjecAgent

The benchmark contains separate test cases for different indirect prompt injection scenarios.

