# How These Datasets Support This Research

The datasets and benchmarks above can be used at different stages of an agent safety pipeline.

### Objective Validation

Agent tasks can be evaluated to determine whether the agent correctly interprets the user's intended objective.

### Runtime Monitoring

Agent trajectories can be monitored to identify unsafe actions, unexpected tool calls, or deviations from the intended task.

### Safety Evaluation

Benchmarks such as Agent-SafetyBench and AgentHarm can be used to evaluate the overall safety of an agent.

### Tool-Use Safety

ToolEmu, AgentDojo, and τ-Bench provide environments for evaluating agents that interact with external tools.

### Prompt Injection Defense

AgentDojo and InjecAgent can be used to evaluate whether an agent can resist malicious instructions originating from external data.

### Rollback Research

These benchmarks can also be extended with checkpoint and rollback mechanisms.

For example:

```text
Agent receives objective
        ↓
Agent creates plan
        ↓
Safety validation
        ↓
Checkpoint
        ↓
Tool execution
        ↓
Runtime monitoring
        ↓
Unsafe behavior detected?
      ↙       ↘
    YES        NO
     ↓          ↓
  Rollback    Continue
     ↓
Safe state restored
```

This allows researchers to evaluate not only whether an agent makes an unsafe decision, but also whether the system can **detect the failure, interrupt execution, and recover to a previously verified safe state**.

